  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1247",
      "html": "<p>Noturus nocturnus Jordan &amp; Gilbert, 1886. Common names: Freckled Madtom, Fregnet Giftmalle (Denmark). Family: Ictaluridae. Size: 15 cm SL. IUCN Red List category: Least Concern (last assessed 2012).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Common name: Freckled madtom. Family: Ictaluridae. Adult size: 12 cm. Temperature: 16–23°C. pH: 6.5–7.2.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1247",
      "html": "<p>North America: Mississippi River basin from northern Illinois to Louisiana, and from eastern Kentucky to central Kansas and Oklahoma in the USA; Gulf Slope drainages from Mobile Bay in Alabama to Guadalupe River in Texas, USA.</p><p>Type locality: Saline River, near Benton railroad bridge, at Benton, Saline County, Arkansas, U.S.A.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>North America: Mississippi River basin from northern Illinois to Louisiana, and from eastern Kentucky to central Kansas and Oklahoma in the USA; Gulf Slope drainages from Mobile Bay in Alabama to Guadalupe River in Texas, USA.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Inhabits sand-gravel riffles and runs near debris and among tree roots along undercut banks in creeks to large rivers.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>The madtoms are well suited to aquarium life. Provide river gravel and smooth rounded stones for hiding places during the day. A power filter at one end of the tank will provide the conditions of a flowing stream for these catfish. As with most of this genus, handle with care: the pectoral fin spine has a poison gland at its base.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Feeds on aquatic insect immatures dominated by mayflies, caddisflies, midges, and blackflies. In the aquarium they like most meaty food, such as cut-up earth worms, frozen bloodworm and prawns. They will also take tablet and pellet foods. A varied diet will keep them in optimum health.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Can be housed with other North American fishes such as minnows, darters and shiners.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1247",
      "html": "<p>Unreported. There is no breeding report.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1247",
      "html": "<p>Etymology: from the Greek notos, meaning back, and oura, meaning tail, in reference to the confluent adipose and caudal fins. The specific epithet refers to its black (nocturnus = night) colour.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Description: This species is moderately robust. The head is depressed, rounded above with the upper jaw overhanging the lower. 4 pairs of barbels. Pectoral fin spine with anterior edge smooth, posterior edge roughened or with 3–5 serrations. Anal fin is short, 15–20 rays. Colouration: Body has a uniform colour of a brown back with the sides lighter with dark freckles. The belly is yellowish. The bases of the median fins are dusky and lighter towards the edges, margins are clear.</p><p>Etymology: nocturnus means nocturnal, referring to the dark colouration.</p><p>IUCN Red List: Least Concern in view of the large extent of occurrence, large number of subpopulations, large population size, and lack of major threats.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "planetcatfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.planetcatfish.com/common/species.php?species_id=1247",
      "html": "<p>Proceedings of the United States National Museum v. 9 (no. 549) (for 14 Aug. 1886), pp 6.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ictaluridae/noturus_nocturnus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2009. FishBase. World Wide Web electronic publication. www.fishbase.org, version (05/2012). NatureServe. 2013. Noturus nocturnus. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2013. The Audubon Society Field guide to North American Fishes, Whales &amp; Dolphins. Alfred A. Knopf, New York. 848 p.</p>"
    }
  ],
